WEBVTT

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.240
Just having the browser on people's devices,

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:04.240
and you know, the browser is very well optimized.

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:05.760
It's written in Rust maybe.

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:06.080
I don't know.

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:11.120
If you actually think at the scale of our industry of Python,

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:14.480
all of the servers that you need to have to power this software--

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:16.640
Django in Rust?

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:18.960
You know, we actually had this as one of our April's

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:21.040
fools blog post ideas.

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:22.880
And currently it's written in Python.

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:25.360
And we think it would make a lot of sense for that

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:29.120
specifically to be written in another language, like Rust.

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:33.040
We know everyone loves to convert things to Rust these days.

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:35.040
So far, we talked about web.

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:37.200
There's the whole AI space.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:39.520
Lots to say about the carbon footprints of AI.

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:41.280
And when I say those three things, again,

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:43.520
like the big elephant in the room is AI,

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:45.760
I think we should expect way more transparency,

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:48.720
not from open source developers, but from those businesses

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:50.160
that build those models.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:53.360
Did you know that every website has a carbon footprint?

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:55.920
What if even the smallest code changes in tools we all use

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:57.600
could have a big impact?

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:00.160
In this episode, I talk with Thibaud Colas.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:02.800
His product lead in engineering manager at Torchbox,

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:04.480
product lead for Wagtail, and president

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:06.400
of the Django Software Foundation.

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:08.320
We talked about digital sustainability

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:14.320
and what we can do to make the web greener.

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:18.240
Python developers are aware and care a lot about performance,

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:21.280
but what about carbon footprint?

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:23.120
Yeah, good question.

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:26.800
Well, when you think of the impact of your software,

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:30.080
think first of all as a Python dev, you think of the users,

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:32.800
maybe devs, maybe people that aren't technical,

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:34.560
that the software supports.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:38.640
But you don't necessarily think about the impacts on the planet.

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:41.440
And I think that's because technology, software,

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:43.280
you know, we don't really see where it runs.

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:44.800
It's very intangible.

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:50.240
But if you actually think at the scale of our industry of Python,

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:52.560
all of the servers that you need to have to power

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:56.240
this software and how much energy is needed

00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:00.160
both to create those devices and keep them on the internet,

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:02.000
it's a lot of energy.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:06.160
And even though it might not be flying twice a day,

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:09.040
levels of energy, it definitely adds up.

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:11.760
So, yeah, when it comes to running Python code,

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:14.160
you think of how it is, you think of the CPU,

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:18.960
and that CPU needs the server and needs the electricity to power it.

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:19.360
Right.

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:22.960
And is this question that matters to a lot of developers?

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:26.400
They often get those questions or any discussions.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:30.000
So, you think this is an niche topic that people don't really talk about.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:31.920
I ask, because this starts with performance,

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:34.880
and every other Python dev asks,

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:36.960
oh, should we be rewriting this in Rust?

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:40.640
Because every dev also cares about performance.

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:45.840
But very few people actually think about the carbon impacts.

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:48.400
And I don't think it's necessarily because they don't care.

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:49.600
It's just not aware.

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:53.600
When they think of carbon, they think of flying, maybe the car,

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:55.920
maybe if they have a company that thinks about those things,

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:58.560
maybe they think of commuting, the office space,

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:01.200
they don't necessarily think of the sites and apps

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:03.200
that they build with the software.

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:07.280
So, to me, the type of thing where, once people start to think about this,

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:11.120
they realize that if they are software that are quite a large scale,

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:16.720
maybe the software itself is a bigger deal than all those other aspects.

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:20.320
One example I can give you, Mozilla, the Mozilla Foundation,

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:22.880
they manage the Firefox browser,

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:25.520
and they were one of the first ones to report

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:29.440
not just the carbon footprint of the Mozilla Foundation,

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:34.560
but also of Firefox, the browser, as it's used on people's devices.

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:40.560
And I don't know if you can guess how much the Firefox usage is of Mozilla emissions.

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:44.480
It's 98% of their organization footprints

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:46.720
is just people running Firefox.

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:49.040
So, when you start to think through that...

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:50.800
The browser and do nothing there.

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:52.880
Just having the browser on people's devices.

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:54.880
And the browser is very well optimized.

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:56.480
It's written in Rust, maybe?

00:03:56.480 --> 00:04:00.480
I don't know, but even with all of that in place,

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:03.040
it has a big energy footprints.

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:05.200
And it matters if you care about the climate,

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:06.960
but also if you have it on your phone

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:09.760
and you want a longer battery life, simple like that.

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:14.480
And what is the impact on the web software and websites in general

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:15.520
on carbon footprint?

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:17.680
Because when talking about carbon footprint,

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:21.360
we often talk about industries,

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:23.600
flying corporations,

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:28.960
but I wonder how much impact does software have on it?

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:31.680
Yeah, for websites specifically,

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:35.360
there is this quite well established methodology

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:39.120
called Sustainable Web Design SWD.

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:42.560
And it divides website emissions in three categories.

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:46.560
It considers the servers, how much energy they use,

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:49.760
the user's devices that display the website's pages

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:51.840
and the network in between.

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:54.320
And you realize that when you start to crunch through the numbers,

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:56.000
that's all those things matter.

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:58.480
And sexually, the user's devices

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:00.720
that have the highest emissions.

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:04.800
Because if you think about it when a server creates a page,

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:08.400
it can cache it and display the same page for a thousand people.

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:09.680
But when someone opens the page,

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:12.240
they're going to do that once, maybe twice and so on.

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:14.240
So that adds up pretty fast.

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:17.120
For Django, for example, I'm involved with the Django Foundation

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:19.280
as the president this year.

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:26.240
And I estimate there's only 500 to 500,000 to a million Django projects

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:27.360
out there.

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:29.840
And those projects, the average footprint is only

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:32.800
other of a ton of CO2 per year.

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:36.240
So you can clearly see how fast the numbers add up,

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:41.920
even though people don't necessarily think of Django as an energy intensive.

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:47.440
So many websites that run on Django, it would be improved Django itself.

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:50.000
Then the overall impact would be very big.

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:54.160
And what is the current state of carbon footprint

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:56.960
in the Django ecosystem?

00:05:56.960 --> 00:06:00.160
The current state is, I think, first and foremost,

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:05.280
we have to have more developers invested into this and thinking about it

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:08.320
and more companies as well.

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:11.200
There are quite a few very big Django users

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:14.880
out there that have quite clear climate commitments.

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:19.840
I think of people like the NHS in the UK, the biggest UK employer.

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:24.000
They have a very large sites with billions of billions, sorry, of page use per year.

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:26.320
And they have climate commitments.

00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:30.560
So it's really crucial for those people that the tech they select

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:32.320
missed those commitments.

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:34.400
And when it comes to open source contributors,

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:38.160
I think it's worth saying that they definitely have a clear stake

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:41.200
and opportunities to reduce that footprint.

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:44.000
So when you think of individual actions, there's activism,

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:46.800
but they could also be contributing to a product like Django

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:51.120
and reducing its footprints by improving its performance.

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:55.680
And what are some gaps in Django or some areas for improvements?

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:56.800
Oh, there's so many.

00:06:56.800 --> 00:07:02.400
I mean, you know, I think it's still quite early days for this industry.

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:07.520
I think the biggest gap to start for starters is just having those methodologies

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:09.600
to measure emissions.

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:11.600
So far, we talked about web.

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:13.840
There's the whole AI space.

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:16.000
Lots to say about the carbon footprint of AI.

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:19.040
But one thing that's really clear is that it's very easy to measure.

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:22.160
And I think people should start to ask for those measurements a bit more.

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:23.760
So how is it measured?

00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:28.320
If you have a website somewhere and I deploy it to cloud or anywhere,

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:32.800
how do I measure carbon footprint of my website?

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:37.360
The simplest way to get started if you want like a kind of approximation,

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:40.000
is to measure the weight of the pages.

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:43.840
And the sad truth is the number, the weight of the pages across the web is going up

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:45.680
and up and up every year.

00:07:45.680 --> 00:07:49.760
And that's really just, you know, heavy, more heavy weight pages are slower to load

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:51.360
on your own devices.

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:52.960
So there's that to get started.

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:55.520
Then if you want more precise measurements,

00:07:55.520 --> 00:07:59.520
you're going to look at measuring the CPU usage of the site,

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:02.880
measuring the memory use, how many servers are needed,

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:06.960
and you need to start to investigate also not just like how much resources,

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:09.520
but also where in the world those resources are used.

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:13.760
So you know, if you have a data center that's powered by uniable energy,

00:08:13.760 --> 00:08:18.960
it's going to emit much less carbon than one powered by gas, paraphones.

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:23.600
So going back to Django, what are some future plans about it?

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:26.800
Because you said there are lots of things that can be improved.

00:08:26.800 --> 00:08:29.760
Do you have any road make or any plan to improve it?

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:30.800
Or...

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:35.280
My wish, my dream, would be to get the whole Python community

00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:37.840
to align on this and make it happen.

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:42.640
For Django itself, I'm actually part of another project called the Wactel CMS,

00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:45.120
where we did produce a roadmap.

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:48.480
So Wagtail is just one of many Django packages.

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:53.360
Wagtail powers on the order of 20,000 sites, including the NHS one.

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:57.120
And for Wagtail, we spend some time creating a roadmap of 10 items,

00:08:57.120 --> 00:09:00.640
which we think are the most important to make progress on this.

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:03.920
And those 10 items, roughly speaking, I believe there is six

00:09:03.920 --> 00:09:06.640
that are improvements to Wagtail itself.

00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:09.840
There is the rest that are about awareness in the community.

00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:13.360
So something I'd like to see for Django in particular,

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:16.960
we have ways to make Django much smaller and still very useful.

00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:20.240
You know, these days, people really like frameworks like FastAPI

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:24.160
that are much smaller in footprints, from the series energy use,

00:09:24.160 --> 00:09:25.760
but just amount of code.

00:09:25.760 --> 00:09:28.160
And I think Django could address that need better

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:30.560
and lower is footprint at the same time.

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:32.080
And what about languages?

00:09:32.080 --> 00:09:35.120
Different languages have different footprint, right?

00:09:35.120 --> 00:09:36.080
For sure.

00:09:36.080 --> 00:09:41.200
And Python is one of the worst ones, isn't it?

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:45.840
Does it mean that we should consider using more other languages?

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:47.280
It's a good question.

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:51.360
I definitely think that, you know, if you do data processing,

00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:56.160
it makes sense to convert some of that code into C or Rust.

00:09:56.160 --> 00:09:59.920
We know everyone loves to convert things to Rust these days.

00:09:59.920 --> 00:10:02.720
And, but the thing is, we already know that,

00:10:02.720 --> 00:10:05.200
because we already care about improving performance,

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:07.360
yeah, about reducing costs.

00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:09.440
But I think when it comes to reducing the footprints,

00:10:09.440 --> 00:10:12.960
there's lots of room to do things in Python still.

00:10:12.960 --> 00:10:15.760
So if you want an example, one I really like,

00:10:15.760 --> 00:10:20.320
is image processing and reducing the size of images.

00:10:20.320 --> 00:10:23.200
Of course, you're going to use a C library to do that.

00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:24.960
That's very well optimized.

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:27.760
But the integration from that to Python,

00:10:27.760 --> 00:10:30.080
we have to have it written in Python.

00:10:30.080 --> 00:10:33.680
And having those integrations with those newer formats for images

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:37.760
is a type of thing that makes a difference.

00:10:37.760 --> 00:10:41.840
So you're not planning to rewrite Django in Rust?

00:10:41.840 --> 00:10:44.640
You know, we actually had this as one of our April's fools,

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:46.240
blog post ideas.

00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:48.880
And we thought it was just like slightly too,

00:10:48.880 --> 00:10:51.040
you know, it might feel quite real for people.

00:10:51.040 --> 00:10:54.480
So I guess for Django in Rust, we actually have concrete plans.

00:10:54.480 --> 00:10:57.200
I can't say like plans as in we have any commitments.

00:10:57.200 --> 00:10:59.360
But definitely interests.

00:10:59.360 --> 00:11:01.760
I don't know if you know when it comes to serving Django,

00:11:01.760 --> 00:11:03.840
you combine it with a web server.

00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:06.480
There's one called Granian, as written in Rust,

00:11:06.480 --> 00:11:11.360
and has a fair bit more optimized footprints than others.

00:11:11.360 --> 00:11:14.320
And another thing is the Django template language,

00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:16.000
currently is written in Python.

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:18.880
And we think it would make a lot of sense for that specifically

00:11:18.880 --> 00:11:22.160
to be written in another language like Rust.

00:11:22.160 --> 00:11:25.680
So Lilly Foote, one of Django's steering council members,

00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:26.640
she's working on that.

00:11:26.640 --> 00:11:29.280
At the moment, I believe it's more of a side project.

00:11:29.280 --> 00:11:34.080
But definitely one that we could see moving to Django core in the future.

00:11:34.080 --> 00:11:39.520
So what are some other tips how to reduce it except of Rust?

00:11:39.520 --> 00:11:43.360
I mean, you know, I don't know if in your personal life,

00:11:43.360 --> 00:11:45.600
some people spend lots of time about this,

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:49.840
you know, there's the reduce, reuse, recycle, mantra.

00:11:49.840 --> 00:11:53.280
So definitely this is the right mindset in my opinion.

00:11:53.280 --> 00:11:56.000
And I think I would add one before in our case,

00:11:56.000 --> 00:12:01.120
which is measure just to have a sense of where any change might move the needle.

00:12:01.120 --> 00:12:04.800
And when we say reduce, I think it's really important to be clear that

00:12:04.800 --> 00:12:09.360
we're not necessarily asking people to completely scale down and stop doing technology,

00:12:09.360 --> 00:12:13.520
just thinking of where the tech is most useful, focus on that,

00:12:13.520 --> 00:12:16.560
and the bloats that just makes your device run slower,

00:12:16.560 --> 00:12:20.320
that makes your battery drain faster, like remove those bits.

00:12:20.320 --> 00:12:22.240
And how can we raise awareness?

00:12:22.240 --> 00:12:25.600
Because if you go to conferences, if you read blogs,

00:12:25.600 --> 00:12:30.560
this is not really a topic for mainstream tech media.

00:12:30.560 --> 00:12:34.320
I think it's starting to get mainstream in the context of AI actually,

00:12:34.320 --> 00:12:38.000
because people really see the buildup in data centers,

00:12:38.000 --> 00:12:42.200
and how the big cloud players, you know, they had those quite clear net zero commitments

00:12:42.200 --> 00:12:44.400
that are starting to get watered down a bit.

00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:47.280
And I think that's actually a space where awareness is raising.

00:12:47.280 --> 00:12:50.640
But I guess to your point in the Python world,

00:12:50.640 --> 00:12:54.960
I think what actually works is aligning this with performance concerns.

00:12:54.960 --> 00:12:59.040
So I mentioned images, things like the A with image formats,

00:12:59.040 --> 00:13:00.800
it has SEO benefits.

00:13:00.800 --> 00:13:04.400
It makes your users really happy if the sites load faster.

00:13:04.400 --> 00:13:06.080
So lots of good reasons to do this.

00:13:06.080 --> 00:13:09.120
And the carbon, lower carbon is just one of them.

00:13:09.120 --> 00:13:13.440
I think the other thing is things like Python performance improvements.

00:13:13.440 --> 00:13:17.840
So in Python 3.11, I believe, we had quite good performance bumps

00:13:17.840 --> 00:13:20.560
across pretty much any Python projects.

00:13:20.560 --> 00:13:24.400
When we talk about those things, also talking about the reduced energy

00:13:24.400 --> 00:13:29.440
use, which again is good for costs in the servers, but also the climates.

00:13:29.440 --> 00:13:33.840
And I don't know if you follow with the latest 3.14 Python developments,

00:13:33.840 --> 00:13:36.880
there's a free threading walk in the way.

00:13:36.880 --> 00:13:38.800
That as well could have a big impact.

00:13:38.800 --> 00:13:45.040
So I think combining that walk with benchmarks on energy use, go a long way.

00:13:45.040 --> 00:13:48.560
And if I would like to learn more just to get started,

00:13:48.560 --> 00:13:51.920
or if I were a maintainer having my own project,

00:13:51.920 --> 00:13:55.120
where can I read about it more?

00:13:55.120 --> 00:14:01.040
My favorite resource by far is knowing the Python world.

00:14:01.040 --> 00:14:04.400
You come for the tech and you stay for the community, I think.

00:14:04.400 --> 00:14:09.760
And there's a community for software practitioners who care about climates.

00:14:09.760 --> 00:14:12.240
It's called climate action tech.

00:14:12.240 --> 00:14:15.280
It's an online community that's very easy to join, completely free.

00:14:15.280 --> 00:14:19.600
And you're just in this server with like-minded people

00:14:19.600 --> 00:14:22.080
to chat about those different topics.

00:14:22.080 --> 00:14:23.840
And you're there with the experts,

00:14:23.840 --> 00:14:26.960
and other people who are interested, and who share the right resources.

00:14:26.960 --> 00:14:29.760
So climate action tech would be my go-to.

00:14:29.760 --> 00:14:33.360
And I think the other one I would recommend is lots of people these days.

00:14:33.360 --> 00:14:34.960
They care about AI.

00:14:34.960 --> 00:14:39.280
There's excellent reporting on the carbon footprint of AI from Stanford,

00:14:39.280 --> 00:14:42.080
but I think people will find it really interesting.

00:14:42.080 --> 00:14:47.360
And if I would like to contribute somehow, is there anything that I can do?

00:14:47.360 --> 00:14:51.120
I would love for us all to coordinate more.

00:14:51.120 --> 00:14:57.280
And in my mind, the best way would be for you to find those folks on the climate action tech,

00:14:57.280 --> 00:15:02.480
get in touch with them and discuss for your specific projects what moves the needle.

00:15:02.480 --> 00:15:05.040
So right now, if I think of contributing,

00:15:05.040 --> 00:15:09.280
there is this, I don't know if you know the W3C who do accessibility standards.

00:15:09.280 --> 00:15:11.920
They're also working on a sustainability standard.

00:15:11.920 --> 00:15:15.360
And I think they could really use feedback from web devs.

00:15:15.360 --> 00:15:18.640
And being able to be part of those standards is also a big opportunity for people.

00:15:18.640 --> 00:15:22.400
It's quite a new field. I think it's a lot of room to grow your query in that.

00:15:22.400 --> 00:15:25.520
So yeah, climate action tech, reviewing those standards,

00:15:25.520 --> 00:15:28.480
giving the feedback and just connecting with people.

00:15:28.480 --> 00:15:32.480
And you mentioned that there are some standards, but is there anyway,

00:15:32.480 --> 00:15:40.160
how can we make open source developers or in general, developers accountable for the footprint

00:15:40.160 --> 00:15:43.520
of the tools they create?

00:15:43.520 --> 00:15:45.600
Accountability, that's a tough topic, Mia.

00:15:45.600 --> 00:15:49.120
I don't know if we can definitely make people accountable because you know,

00:15:49.120 --> 00:15:50.320
when you put software

00:15:50.320 --> 00:15:54.960
out there as open source, you don't necessarily think a lot about

00:15:54.960 --> 00:15:58.480
any commitments to your users afterwards.

00:15:58.480 --> 00:16:01.200
I think it's definitely worth thinking of what you can do,

00:16:01.200 --> 00:16:07.040
but I would say it as in more think through how people will use the software and try and set them

00:16:07.040 --> 00:16:13.040
on the happy path. So to me, if you want to start to move totally towards accountability,

00:16:13.040 --> 00:16:16.880
what's the most helpful actually is just transparency and sharing

00:16:16.880 --> 00:16:18.480
the results and sharing the numbers.

00:16:18.480 --> 00:16:24.800
So for Wactel, for example, we try hard to publish our footprints, not just of our project

00:16:24.800 --> 00:16:29.360
organization, but also the Wactel sites up there, but we start on a yearly basis.

00:16:29.360 --> 00:16:33.360
I would expect the same from Django because we have quite a serious foundation.

00:16:33.360 --> 00:16:36.960
And eventually I'd love to see the same from Python as well.

00:16:36.960 --> 00:16:40.960
And when I say those three things, again, like the big elephant in the room is AI,

00:16:40.960 --> 00:16:44.880
I think we should expect way more transparency, not from open source developers,

00:16:44.880 --> 00:16:48.560
but from those businesses that build those models that change our society.

00:16:48.560 --> 00:16:55.120
And is there anything that we can do in regards to businesses, big tech, corporations,

00:16:55.120 --> 00:16:56.480
cloud providers?

00:16:56.480 --> 00:17:02.720
Yeah, I think it's just, you know, understanding the benefits of those providers and the tools

00:17:02.720 --> 00:17:05.040
they provide and of their AI models,

00:17:05.040 --> 00:17:09.680
but also understanding the impact on the planet, the society,

00:17:09.680 --> 00:17:12.800
and I guess asking for more, raising your standards.

00:17:12.800 --> 00:17:17.360
When it comes to AI, for example, I try and push people towards local models

00:17:17.360 --> 00:17:21.440
because it really forces them to be more aware of the, I guess,

00:17:21.440 --> 00:17:26.640
how big of a server you have to have to run them and also how much energy it takes to run them.

00:17:26.640 --> 00:17:31.840
And once you have that kind of feedback loop of actually wanting to reduce those numbers,

00:17:31.840 --> 00:17:33.920
you naturally do it.

00:17:33.920 --> 00:17:34.960
Thank you for coming.

00:17:34.960 --> 00:17:37.440
Thanks for having me.
